### Reasoning

The problem asks for the minimum cost to build a supervisor-subordinate hierarchy. We are given the qualifications of each employee and the cost of each potential supervisor-subordinate relationship.  A key constraint is that the supervisor's qualification must be greater than the subordinate's.

The core idea is to explore all possible root nodes (employees who don't have a supervisor) and then recursively build the hierarchy from that root.  For each root, we iterate through the available edges (applications) and try to add them to the hierarchy if they connect the current node to an unvisited node, thus ensuring that each employee has at most one supervisor.

The `find_min_cost` function implements the recursive exploration. It takes the current root node, the set of visited nodes (employees already in the hierarchy), and the current cost as input.  If all employees have been visited, it returns the current cost. Otherwise, it iterates through the available edges originating from the current root. If an edge leads to an unvisited employee, it recursively calls itself with the new root (the subordinate), the updated visited set, and the increased cost.  The minimum cost found across all possible paths is returned.

The main part of the solution iterates through each possible root node, calls `find_min_cost`, and keeps track of the minimum total cost found so far. If no valid hierarchy can be built (i.e., the minimum cost remains infinity), it outputs -1. Otherwise, it outputs the minimum cost.

